<td id="A2.T4.2.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A2.T4.2.7.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">0.703</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span><math id="A2.T4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max@1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max@1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.T4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max@128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max@128</annotation></semantics></math> metrics for the pure RLVR.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we provide additional results for pure RLVR with extended training.
Table <a href="#A2.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Appendix B Additional RLVR results ‣ The Best of N Worlds:Aligning Reinforcement Learning with Best-of-N Sampling via max@k Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> presents results with more epochs and extra PPO steps.
As can be seen from the results, training with more epochs consistently improves the metric.
The best <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max@1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max@1</annotation></semantics></math> is attained by four epochs pure RL training for both datasets.
For CodeContests dataset, as expected, we observe degradation of the <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max@128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max@128</annotation></semantics></math> metric even with extra training.
At the same time, for LiveCodeBench dataset, the best <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max@128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max@128</annotation></semantics></math> is attained by the RL with three PPO steps.
This might indicate that the problem of diversity after RL training is less pronounced for certain datasets.
Nevertheless, the decrease of <span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">max@k</span> metric might still be present for larger <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> values.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Technical Details</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span><span id="A3.SS1.2" class="ltx_text">pass@k</span> and <span id="A3.SS1.3" class="ltx_text">max@k</span> evaluation</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the <span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">pass@k</span> and <span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">max@k</span> metrics, we sample <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>256</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256</annotation></semantics></math> completions for each prompt.
For <span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text">pass@k</span>, we adopt the unbiased estimator from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2021)</a></cite>:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{pass@k}=1-\frac{\binom{n-c}{k}}{\binom{n}{k}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>pass@k</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{pass@k}=1-\frac{\binom{n-c}{k}}{\binom{n}{k}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the total number of completions and <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> the number of correct ones. Intuitively, the estimator computes the fraction of subsets of size <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> that contain at least one correct completion, i.e., <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n}{k}-\binom{n-c}{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n}{k}-\binom{n-c}{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, normalized by the total number of subsets <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n}{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n}{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
An analogous estimator can be derived for <span id="A3.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">max@k</span> by averaging the maximum reward within each subset of size <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of generated samples, and let <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}\leq r_{2}\leq\dots\leq r_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}\leq r_{2}\leq\dots\leq r_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote their rewards.
Then <span id="A3.SS1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text">max@k</span> can be estimated as:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{max@k}=\frac{1}{\binom{n}{k}}\sum_{i=k}^{n}\binom{i-1}{k-1}r_{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>max@k</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{max@k}=\frac{1}{\binom{n}{k}}\sum_{i=k}^{n}\binom{i-1}{k-1}r_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where the coefficient <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{i-1}{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{i-1}{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> counts the number of subsets of size <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> whose maximum reward is <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We show our implementation in Listing <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="LST1" class="ltx_float ltx_lstlisting ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:377.6pt;">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
